Kaian proposed a new geometry: generosity as a tide, not a tally. They forged “Circles of Return,” where returns could be time, skill, care, or opportunity—so long as they sustained every anchor in Hollowtide. Apprentices learned to carve rails, cooks prepared communal suppers, caretakers tended herb gardens, elders recited harbor histories at dusk. The poet’s appetite cooled, replaced by a quiet chorus that threaded through every doorway.

As reciprocity deepened, Hollowtide thrived: lanterns glowed along the quay, boats carried cargo with trust, markets sang with gratitude. The coast learned a simple truth: when gifts travel outward with tenderness, the current returns as a chorus of renewed life, inviting tomorrow’s wanderers to belong.
